 {:seon.task/name "grep-ranks-definitions-first"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood (gap 3): `(my.agent/grep \"summary\")` answers about 30 names, unranked and without the matching line, at about 350 ms and 240 MB a call. Rank definitions whose name holds the text first, then docstrings, then bodies, and give each its matching line through the one printer. Measure before and after on the three fit requests' words; if the cost does not fall, find where the 240 MB goes first (a cost proof). Plan (2026-10-25, learning done, nothing run while the owner's machine is loaded): the cost is the query itself, `[(clojure.string/includes? ?src ?s)]` as a Datalog predicate over every `:form/src` (the query engine binds and materializes each tuple); one `:aevt` scan of `:form/src` with `str/index-of` on each value, then the matching forms' `:form/defines`, is the same answer without the engine (measure both at n = the program, ~1,800 forms). Ranked: a var whose name holds s first, then one whose `:var/doc` holds it, then the rest, each `{:var :line}` with the first matching line of its source (or its doc), the line printed by the one printer like any value. The contract changes from a vector of names to that vector of maps; its one caller in src is none (the help page and seon.repl-test/the-help-page-runs call it). Proofs: ranking on made forms (name before doc before body; the line is the matching one), and the cost proof linear in forms with a constant per form well under today's (bytes measured before and after)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/grep-ranks-names-before-bodies seon.docs-cost-test/grep-costs-its-forms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-name-that-is-no-var-says-so"
  :seon.task/plan "The live rounds (notes/live-2026-10-25.md, rounds 4-9: what still fails) ended runs :no-progress on calls whose answers gave nothing new, among them `(my.agent/callers 'seon.proof/status)`: a symbol where the registry holds the key `:seon.proof/status`. ```my.agent/callers``` and ```my.agent/callees``` took the symbol as it was and answered [] for a name that is no var, which reads as no callers (AGENTS section 9: unknown is not empty), so the agent asked again. Now a symbol that names no var on the branch nor in the process (a library's var is one) answers :my.agent/no-var, naming it, with the form for the registry key of that name when the branch has one (an attribute, a config key or a schema entry). Replaces the silent []."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-name-that-is-no-var-says-so}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-missing-name-says-the-nearest"
  :seon.task/plan "The live rounds' repeats (notes/live-2026-10-25.md; live-agents' jolt/repeat-causes) re-sent a guessed name after `source` answered \"Source not found ... If your plan names it as new, write it\" (r1, r7 twice, r9), and after a missing namespace or \"No such var\" with no alternative (r8 twice, r9). Two producers answered a missing name: ```my.agent/not-found``` for source and ```my.agent/no-var``` for callers and callees. Now ```my.agent/no-var``` is the one answer, and source prints its message. It names the nearest existing names by Jolt's own did-you-mean ranking, `jolt.analyzer/suggestions-for` (made public on seantempesta/jolt jolt/suggestions-for-public): the definitions of the name's namespace, or the nearest namespaces when that namespace is none. It gives the registry key's form when there is one, and says write it only when the task's plan cites the name (read by `seon.ref/names` as it stands, since :seon.task/references is derived only when the task is declared). Deletes ```my.agent/not-found```; no second edit distance in src."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-missing-name-says-the-nearest seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers seon.repl-test/a-name-that-is-no-var-says-so}}]
